# Homebrew formula for Otis, rendered by .github/workflows/release.yml and
# committed to otis-http/homebrew-otis as Formula/otis.rb.
#
# @@VERSION@@, @@URL@@ and @@SHA256@@ are substituted from the published
# release. The sha256 is taken from the artifact after it is uploaded, not
# from a local rebuild, so it is the hash of the file a user will actually
# download.
#
# A formula and not a cask, which is unusual for something with a window, and
# it works because Otis is one binary: `otis` with no arguments opens the app
# and `otis run` is the CLI. So one `brew install` delivers both, and — the
# reason to prefer it on macOS — Homebrew does not set the quarantine
# attribute on what it downloads, so an unsigned Otis installed this way never
# raises Gatekeeper.
#
# What a formula does not give you is a bundle: no Dock icon, no Launchpad
# entry, and no .http file association, since all three come from Otis.app's
# Info.plist. The DMG is for that. docs/RELEASING.md keeps the two straight.
#
# macOS only, deliberately. The Linux binary is dynamically linked against
# WebKitGTK 6.0, so the loader needs those libraries present before it will
# start at all — even to run a CLI command that never opens a window. A Linux
# Homebrew user is better served by the deb, the rpm, or
# `go install -tags otis_cli`.
class Otis < Formula
  desc "File-based HTTP client: a collection is a directory of .http files"
  homepage "https://github.com/otis-http/otis"
  version "@@VERSION@@"
  url "@@URL@@"
  sha256 "@@SHA256@@"
  license "MIT"

  depends_on :macos

  def install
    bin.install "otis"
  end

  def caveats
    <<~EOS
      This installs the `otis` command, which is also the app: run `otis` with
      no arguments to open the window, or `otis --help` for the commands.

      Homebrew does not quarantine its downloads, so there is no Gatekeeper
      prompt even though Otis is unsigned.

      For a Dock icon, a Launchpad entry and the .http file association,
      install Otis.app from the DMG instead:
        https://github.com/otis-http/otis/releases
    EOS
  end

  test do
    # The version substring, not "otis #{version}": the stamped version keeps
    # the tag's leading "v" (`otis v0.2.0`) while the formula's version does
    # not, so anchoring on the prefix would fail on every release.
    assert_match version.to_s, shell_output("#{bin}/otis --version")
    assert_match "Available Commands", shell_output("#{bin}/otis --help")
  end
end
